Code Review

﻿﻿﻿Commit essential changes to your project as you go. When you’ve finished working on your project, push the branch and make a pull request (PR) in GitHub. Copy the URL of the PR and submit it below. 





If you are unfamiliar with pull-requests, then you should definitely check this walkthrough of how to create a branch and open pull requests.


Once you submit your own PR, you will get PR’s of two other TUMO Labs students, which you should code review. For each of their mini-projects, clone the repo and run it locally to test that it works correctly. Then review the code, leaving comments in the PR when you have a suggestion or question. If you don’t know where to start, here are some things to look for (from Google’s code review guide):



The overall design — Do the interactions of various pieces of code make sense?
Functionality — Does it do what the developer intended?
Complexity — Is it more complex than it should be?
Naming — Did the developer pick good names for everything?
Good Things — If you see something nice, tell the developer, especially when they addressed one of your comments in a great way.


You can then submit your comments when you either approve the PR or ask for changes. 



Two people will also review your PR! When they do, you will get an email notification. You can then respond to their comments. To make their suggested changes, commit them to that same feature branch and push them to your GitHub repository. The commit(s) will automatically be added to your original PR, and the reviewers can review the changes again. Once they’ve approved your changes, you can merge the feature branch into the main one and close the PR.
